        'en' => [
            'title' => 'The most useful AI of the week did not talk. It drew a map.',
            'image_alt' => 'A dark purple desk with a glowing teal network of connected points. In the centre one point is missing, leaving an empty dashed circle. A closed notebook on the left, a stethoscope on the right.',
            'excerpt' => 'At a Texas medical school, an AI working over a curriculum database found an objective that nobody taught and nobody tracked. The AI patient from the same week showed less: that it can be run and that students want to work alone first. Two results, two different weights of evidence.',
            'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Twenty-five programme objectives, nine clinical clerkships. Twenty-four objectives had a documented place in the clerkships. One had none: nobody taught it, and nobody knew that nobody taught it.</p>
<p>The gap was not found by an accreditation panel or by an unhappy student. It was found by a query against a database. On 12 November 2026 the Texas A&amp;M medical school described in JMIR Medical Education the system it introduced for curriculum governance in the 2025 to 2026 academic year: <a href="https://doi.org/10.2196/107727"><em>An AI-Integrated Relational Curriculum Mapping Framework for Alignment and Transparency in Medical Education</em></a>. Session learning objectives from all 16 preclerkship courses and all clinical clerkships are mapped to programme and course objectives, to codes of the USMLE licensing examination, to keywords and to the levels of Bloom's taxonomy. A language model proposes the mapping and an expert confirms it. Only verified records enter the relational database, and a second AI agent works on top of it, producing a report on request: what repeats, what complements, what is missing.</p>
<p>The results are unglamorous, which is why they persuade. For two courses the system found 50 pairs of overlapping objectives within minutes. Across all 16 courses it showed pathology coverage of 42% against a target range of 45% to 55%. And in the clerkships it found that one orphaned objective.</p>
<p>The most honest number in the study is a different one. Three course directors, blinded, coded a sample of 30 objectives. Agreement with the database was almost perfect for broad domains, κ = 0.94. For exact codes it fell to κ = 0.67. So the system reliably finds the continent and still argues with the experts about the street. It is a useful map, not a finished land register. The authors themselves write that workload savings were not formally measured, and the study says nothing about whether students learn better.</p>
<p>A day later, on 13 November, BMC Medical Education published a second paper, at first sight the more attractive one: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1186/s12909-026-10812-8"><em>Feasibility of an AI-simulated patient encounter delivered in group and individual learning formats for first-year medical students</em></a>. An AI patient for first-year students, once in a group with a teacher, once alone. Of 72 students invited, 19 enrolled, which is 26%. Seven worked in the group, twelve on their own. All completed the encounter, no difference between the formats was statistically significant, and technical problems interrupted some conversations. The cohorts were not randomised, there was a single case, and one author is named as inventor on a patent application covering part of the technology. The study says it can be done. It does not say that anyone learned anything from it.</p>
<p>I still take one thing from it. Seven of the twelve who answered would prefer the order “solo first, then group”. As a working hypothesis it makes sense: first your own attempt, then a shared review of the mistakes with someone who knows where they are usually made.</p>
<p>For nephrology teaching this gives an order opposite to the one a vendor would propose. First the map: every module with a specific objective, a Bloom level, the competence it builds on, sources, test questions and links to what came before and what follows. AI may suggest labels and look for gaps. An expert confirms every placement, and answers are assembled from verified records, not from the model's memory. Only then the patient: a first assessment in acute kidney injury, a complication during dialysis, suspected glomerulonephritis. An independent conversation, feedback against a rubric set in advance, a shared debrief with a teacher.</p>
<p>A talking patient on a screen is easy to demonstrate. A table of objectives is not. But without it we do not even know which conversation the student was supposed to practise. A simulator shows how a student speaks. A map shows what was never spoken about.</p>
<p><em>This text draws on two peer-reviewed papers published on 12 and 13 November 2026. The first is an implementation study without a control group, the second a feasibility pilot with 19 participants. Neither demonstrates better outcomes for students or patients. The proposal for nephrology teaching is my judgement, not a validated programme. An external AI service in such a system means dependence on a particular model and a need to settle data protection by contract.</em></p>
<p><small><em>Sources: Badeau RM, Laury J, Dickey D. An AI-Integrated Relational Curriculum Mapping Framework for Alignment and Transparency in Medical Education: Implementation Study. JMIR Med Educ. 2026;12:e107727. <a href="https://doi.org/10.2196/107727">DOI 10.2196/107727</a>, <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43020473/">PubMed 43020473</a>. Tischkau S, Lynch J, McGinity S, Primous A, Meldrim E, Lynch S, Selinfreund R. Feasibility of an AI-simulated patient encounter delivered in group and individual learning formats for first-year medical students: a two-cohort pilot study. BMC Med Educ. 2026. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1186/s12909-026-10812-8">DOI 10.1186/s12909-026-10812-8</a>.</em></small></p>

